\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}

The explosion of online content has made it impossible for users to manually explore every item to discover their preferences. Consequently, the primary objective of modern recommendation systems has shifted from simply increasing sales to delivering highly accurate and relevant user experiences. However, the volume of user activity data grows so rapidly that even the most experienced systems struggle to extract enough signals for precise preferences, creating a critical challenge known as the \textit{cold-start problem}. This issue manifests in two ways: the \textit{new user problem}, where systems cannot accurately predict preferences due to a lack of interaction history, and the \textit{new item problem}, which occurs when users encounter items with no prior interactions. These challenges are particularly evident in niche areas with sparse data, such as recommending political candidates or candidates for public office.

To address these cold-start issues, Cross Domain Recommendation (CDR) has emerged as a promising multi-domain approach. By transferring useful information from related domains, CDR significantly improves the accuracy of predicting user preferences and the relevance of suggested items. This technique is especially valuable when user interaction data is scarce, such as in recommending political candidates to potential voters. In such contexts, CDR can leverage interaction data from other domains to infer user preferences for political figures.

