\section{Introduction}

Feed-Forward neural networks have proven to be a robust mechanism for solving various Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks, including Dependency Parsing (DP). These networks learn representations of words and dependencies by modeling linguistic patterns through Labeled Dependency edges (MDPS). The primary mechanism driving this learning process is backpropagation, which adjusts network weights to minimize prediction errors.

While feed-forward networks are fully connected, they lack the implicit capability to learn from data in both forward and backward directions. This limitation is particularly problematic for DP, where context information is essential. Specifically, bidirectionality is critical for accurately interpreting words and dependencies. For instance, in the dependency labeled AUX (helping verb) between "will" and "eat" (see Fig. \ref{fig:fig1}), understanding "eat" significantly clarifies "will". Conversely, analyzing the label of the "eat" node also provides valuable context about "will".

